Fix kick bound parsing (issue #36; upstream bug, unfixed as of v1.13).

dispatch_cmd() parsed the `kick` bound with a bare strtoul(): the end
pointer was never checked, so trailing garbage (`kick 1garbage`) was
silently ignored, and a negative bound (`kick -1`) wrapped to a huge
unsigned count. Both returned `KICKED n` and kicked the whole buried
queue instead of replying BAD_FORMAT. The protocol specifies the kick
bound as a non-negative integer and requires malformed commands to
fail with BAD_FORMAT without mutating the queue.

Route the bound through read_u32(), the daemon's own validator used by
the other integer arguments: it rejects a leading minus, requires the
argument to be fully consumed, and range-checks against UINT32_MAX.

--- a/prot.c
+++ b/prot.c
@@ -1288,7 +1288,7 @@
 {
     int r, timeout = -1;
     uint i;
-    uint count;
+    uint32 count;
     Job *j = 0;
     byte type;
     char *size_buf, *delay_buf, *ttr_buf, *pri_buf, *end_buf, *name;
@@ -1614,9 +1614,7 @@
         return;
 
     case OP_KICK:
-        errno = 0;
-        count = strtoul(c->cmd + CMD_KICK_LEN, &end_buf, 10);
-        if (end_buf == c->cmd + CMD_KICK_LEN || errno) {
+        if (read_u32(&count, c->cmd + CMD_KICK_LEN, NULL)) {
             reply_msg(c, MSG_BAD_FORMAT);
             return;
         }
